          What Is a Steel Weight Calculator?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>steel weight calculator</strong> computes the gravitational mass of steel products by calculating their geometric volume and multiplying by the material's specific density:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 font-mono space-y-1">
          <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Fundamental Steel Weight Formula:</div>
          <div>Weight (kg) = Volume (m³) × Material Density (kg/m³)</div>
          <div>Weight (lb) = Volume (in³) × Material Density (0.2836 lb/in³)</div>
          <div class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">For prismatic shapes: Weight = Cross-Sectional Area × Length × Density</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Rebar Weight Calculator & The D²/162 Formula
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          For cylindrical deformed rebar (TMT bars), unit weight per linear meter is computed using the time-tested civil engineering formula:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 font-mono space-y-1">
          <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Rebar Unit Weight Formula:</div>
          <div>Weight per Meter (kg/m) = D² ÷ 162.28  ≈  D² ÷ 162</div>
          <div>Weight per Foot (lb/ft) = (D_mm)² ÷ 241.48  ≈  (Bar Number / 8)² × 2.67 lb/ft</div>
          <div class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">Where D is the nominal rebar diameter in millimeters (mm).</div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Derivation:</strong> The density of mild structural steel is 7,850 kg/m³. The volume of 1 meter of a cylinder of diameter D mm is (π × D² ÷ 4) × 10⁻⁶ × 1 m. Multiplying by 7,850 kg/m³ gives:
          <code>Weight = (3.14159265 × 7850 ÷ 4,000,000) × D² = D² ÷ 162.28 kg/m</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

